05322265625, 0.04107666015625, 0.0283660888671875, -0.0242156982421875, -0.0323486328125, -0.004619598388671875, -0.0167388916015625, 0.009735107421875, 0.033843994140625, -0.002773284912109375, 0.00695037841796875, 0.01806640625, -0.0094757080078125, -0.0185546875, 0.02764892578125, -0.004970550537109375, 0.0413818359375, 0.00676727294921875, 0.0116729736328125, -0.018310546875, 0.03692626953125, 0.0221405029296875, 1.6510486602783203e-05, 0.00360107421875, -0.01271820068359375, -0.01202392578125, 0.0020847320556640625, -0.00252532958984375, -0.016815185546875, 0.031829833984375, 0.0018157958984375, 0.02679443359375, -0.015838623046875, 0.001789093017578125, -0.0047149658203125, -0.005062103271484375, 0.0241241455078125, -0.01788330078125, 0.005016326904296875, -0.0139007568359375, -0.0278778076171875, 0.023529052734375, -0.034149169921875, -0.0303192138671875, 0.0195465087890625, 0.006969451904296875, 0.0186614990234375, 0.00713348388671875, 0.035125732421875, 0.032073974609375, -0.00969696044921875, -0.01263427734375, -0.0015468597412109375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024. A multi-agent AI pipeline was developed and deployed locally to perform report structuring and quality assurance (QA). The pipeline consisted of three primary agents:\n\n1. Sectioning Agent: This agent used a hybrid approach combining regular expression (regex) rules and a local large language model (LLM) to segment reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level. The integration logic was as follows: the regex component first scanned each sentence for explicit section headers and anatomical terms using a predefined list of patterns. If a sentence matched a single, unambiguous regex pattern, it was assigned to the corresponding anatomical section. If a sentence matched multiple patterns or none, or if the context was ambiguous (e.g., overlapping anatomical references or implicit section transitions), the sentence was passed to the LLM (Llama-2-13B, running locally with default parameters and no fine-tuning). The LLM then assigned the sentence to the most appropriate anatomical section based on contextual understanding. For example, if a sentence mentioned \"pleural effusion\" without a clear section header, and regex could not resolve the section, the LLM would use surrounding context to assign it to the \"Pleura\" section. The final section assignment for each sentence prioritized regex-based assignments when unambiguous, and deferred to the LLM only when regex rules were inconclusive or conflicting.\n\n2. Consistency Agent: This agent compared content between the Findings and Impression sections and within sections to detect mismatches. The rule-based logic included: (a) identifying key findings (e.g., \"pulmonary embolism,\" \"liver lesion\") using a curated list of clinical terms; (b) checking for the presence or absence of these findings in both Findings and Impression sections; (c) flagging discrepancies if a key finding appeared in one section but not the other, or if terminology or anatomical references were inconsistent (e.g., \"right renal mass\" in Findings but \"left renal mass\" in Impression). Discrepancies were defined as any mismatch in laterality, organ, or critical finding between sections. The rules were iteratively refined using a development set of 50 reports, with manual review to adjust term lists and logic for improved sensitivity and specificity.\n\n3. QA Agent: This agent identified gender-anatomy conflicts (e.g., references to male anatomy in female patients), and checked for documentation of critical findings and their communication. It used a combination of keyword matching (e.g., searching for \"prostate\" in female patients) and LLM-based contextual analysis to flag potential issues. For critical findings, the agent checked for the presence of communication documentation (e.g., \"results communicated to referring physician\") when a critical finding was detected.\n\nThe agents operated sequentially, with outputs from the Sectioning Agent feeding into the Consistency Agent, followed by the QA Agent. The workflow was orchestrated so that each report passed through all agents, and flagged issues were aggregated for review.\n\nEvaluation Subset Selection: A subset of 45 reports was selected for independent evaluation by two board-certified radiologists. Stratified random sampling was used to ensure proportional representation of all flagged error types (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, and undocumented critical findings) and unflagged reports. The full dataset was divided into strata based on the presence and type of QA flags, and reports were randomly sampled from each stratum in proportion to their prevalence in the full dataset. This approach ensured that each flagged error type and unflagged reports were represented in the evaluation subset according to their frequency, while also guaranteeing inclusion of rare error types.\n\nEvaluation Metrics: \"Correctly restructured\" was defined as the report being segmented into anatomical sections such that each sentence was assigned to the appropriate section without altering the original clinical content or meaning. For example, a sentence about \"hepatic steatosis\" should appear only in the \"Liver\" section, and no sentences should be omitted or moved to an incorrect section. \"Incorrectly restructured\" indicated that the restructuring introduced errors in section assignment (e.g., a lung finding placed in the \"Liver\" section) or altered the intended meaning (e.g., splitting a sentence so that its clinical context was lost). QA performance ratings were assigned on a 3-point scale: \"excellent\" (all relevant issues correctly flagged, no errors or omissions), \"good\" (minor errors or omissions not affecting clinical interpretation, such as a missed typographical inconsistency), and \"fair\" (errors or omissions with potential to affect clinical interpretation, such as missing a critical finding or misclassifying a major discrepancy). Reviewers received standardized written instructions and a training session with example cases to calibrate their assessments. For each report, reviewers independently recorded their judgments according to these definitions.\n\nStatistical Analysis: Reviewer agreement was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic. For key proportions (e.g., percentage of correctly restructured reports, QA performance ratings), 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated using the Wilson score interval. The primary outcome was the proportion of reports correctly restructured by the system, as determined by reviewer consensus. Secondary outcomes included the accuracy of QA flagging (true/false positives for each error type) and inter-rater agreement statistics. Effect sizes were not calculated because the study was descriptive and not designed to compare interventions or groups; the focus was on system performance characterization.\n\nResults:\n\nSystem Performance\n\n- The multi-agent system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while retaining the original report content.\n- The system flagged 90 reports (14.1%) for at least one QA issue:\n    - Section mismatches: 80 reports (12.5%)\n    - Gender-anatomy conflicts: 7 reports (1.1%)\n    - Undocumented communication of critical findings: 3 reports (0.5%)\n- In the full dataset, of the 80 reports flagged for section mismatches, 75 were confirmed as true positives and 5 as false positives by reviewer consensus. Of the 7 reports flagged for gender-anatomy conflicts, 6 were true positives and 1 was a false positive. All 3 reports flagged for undocumented communication of critical findings were true positives.\n\nRadiologist Evaluation\n\n- Of the 45-report evaluation subset, both reviewers agreed that 31 reports (69%; 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2 reports (4%; 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and they disagreed on 12 reports (27%; 95% CI: 15\u201341%). Inter-rater agreement for restructuring was moderate (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.56, p < 0.001).\n- For QA flagging, both reviewers agreed that no clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced in any report.\n- QA performance ratings were \"excellent\" in 24 reports (53%; 95% CI: 38\u201368%), \"good\" in 14 reports (31%; 95% CI: 18\u201346%), and \"fair\" in 7 reports (16%; 95% CI: 7\u201329%). Inter-rater agreement for QA ratings was substantial (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.72, p < 0.001).\n\nDetailed QA Task Results\n\n- Of the 7 reports flagged for gender-anatomy conflicts, both reviewers confirmed 6 as true positives and 1 as a false positive (consensus required for classification).\n- Of the 3 reports flagged for undocumented communication of critical findings, both reviewers agreed all were true positives (consensus required).\n- For section mismatches, 75 of 80 flagged reports were confirmed as true positives by both reviewers (consensus required); the remaining 5 were considered false positives.\n\nLimitations\n\nThis study was limited by its retrospective design and single-institution dataset, which may affect generalizability. The AI system\u2019s performance may vary with different report styles or institutions. The evaluation subset, while stratified, was relatively small (45 reports), which may limit statistical power and the precision of confidence intervals; rare error types may be over- or underrepresented. Report selection may be subject to sampling bias despite stratification. Reviewers were not blinded to the system\u2019s outputs, which could introduce assessment bias and potentially inflate agreement with system decisions. Reviewer assessments, while standardized, may still be subject to individual interpretation, contributing to only moderate inter-rater agreement for some outcomes. The system\u2019s reliance on regex and LLMs may not capture all possible report variations, and rare or institution-specific errors may have been underrepresented.\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system was developed to combine radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow. The system demonstrated effective anatomical sectioning and detection of QA issues, as confirmed by radiologist evaluation and inter-rater agreement statistics. However, the moderate inter-rater agreement and small evaluation subset suggest that further validation in larger and more diverse datasets is warranted. Such systems may have the potential to improve standardization and quality assurance in radiology reporting."}}